-  slug: "twenty-two-page-ids-in-the-tree-refuse-a-check-that-states-every-phase-on",
-  domainSlug: "workspace-package/checks",
-  claim:
-    "`id-is-a-uuid-version-7` states all four phases on, and 22 pages in the tree refuse it. Not one is a version 4 out of `randomId`, so the minter trap that was being hunted has not fired in committed code. 20 are hand-assembled counterfeits under `@akasha/workflow-language` whose third and fourth groups run 5 and 14 hex rather than 4 and 12, and 2 more carry a broken version or variant nibble. The check's own page type carries `Zero At Turning On`, so these 22 keep a judging check from meaning anything, and each of the 22 files is refused the next time a checked road touches it.",
-  evidence:
-    'Measured 2026-09-03 by calling `reasonsIn` from `id-is-a-uuid-version-7.code-check.code.ts:94` over every `.ts` and `.tsx` file beneath `akasha/`, which is the same entry the check itself is built on at `:96`. 80,640 files walked, 80,640 judged, 22 refused. Re-run after my own landing: 80,690 walked, still 22, so that landing added none.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT IS SIGHTED. Seven arms through the same function: a minted version 7 answers clean, while a version 4 out of `crypto.randomUUID()`, a fixed version 4, a version 5, an upper-case version 7, a version 7 carrying variant `2`, and the text `not-a-uuid` each answer with a distinct refusal naming its own mechanism. One pass against six refusals, so this is not a dead instrument reporting agreement.\n\nTHE 22. Twenty are `.module.ts` pages under `akasha/changes/workflow-language/`, landed together in `e1ce2a7555` on 2026-09-02 22:49, `the workflow language comes into akasha as @akasha/workflow-language`. Their ids run `01a06f10-7000-7000-b0000-9d4a2f6c0000e1` through `...-7013-b0013-9d4a2f6c0013e1`: a counter formatted into a uuid template that miscounted the group widths, so they are not uuids at all rather than uuids of the wrong version. The other two landed together in `30deeb0702` on 2026-09-03 11:57: `akasha/alan/harness/monarch/eval/monarch-eval.domain.ts` carries `01a0686a-c033-fae1-cb98-55decd42b2c4`, version `f`, and `akasha/domain-system/findings/pages/an-agents-ceiling-on-flow-is-set-by-conventions-no-prompt-can-carry-honestly.finding.ts` carries `01a0686a-c033-74cd-ed03-623a3033b381`, variant `e`. Those two share the millisecond prefix `01a0686a-c033`, so one run of one hand-rolled minter produced both. All 22 are committed and the worktree is clean for every one of them.\n\nWHY NOTHING CAUGHT THEM. `id-is-a-uuid-version-7.code-check.ts:11-14` states `runsOnPatch`, `runsOnWorktree`, `runsOnDeploy` and `runsOnAudit` all true. But `MECHANICAL` at `asking.module.code.ts:485-489` states `runsChecks: false`, and every landing in this migration goes through `landedMechanically`, so the check never runs at landing time. The landing says so itself: the report line is `a ``change-mechanical`` change runs no check, so this landing was judged by none` (`asking.module.code.ts:40`, `:235`).\n\nTHE HAND-MINTING WAS NEVER NEEDED. `asking.module.code.ts:432-437` calls `mintingOnto` on every landing, mechanical included. Measured against the real root: a new page file stating no `id` comes back filled, `{keys: ["id"], why: "a page being created states none of its own"}`, carrying `01a068e0-1e17-74ab-94ee-8481b5b0b08f`, a version 7. The same file stating a version 4 by hand comes back `filled: []` with the version 4 untouched. So writing an id by hand is what turns the correct minter off, and the mechanical road is what turns the check off. Both halves have to happen, and an author who omits `id` avoids both.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE. `page.page-type.ts:87` says an identity replaced for being no version 7 keeps the last eight hex of the old one, and two findings already on file record replacements that dropped them. Correcting these 22 is therefore a decision about 22 identities rather than a rewrite, and I left them as they are.',
-} as const satisfies Finding
